 "proof_steps": [
  "Let h be the unique row lying in all five P0 quads. Every other nonempty P0-membership row shares at least one P0 quad with h; the exact skeleton has fifteen private rows and three empty rows.",
  "For a profile p let kappa(p)=sum_s binom(p_s,2). P0 has kappa21 and P1 has kappa22, so total column agreements are5*21+3*22=171=binom(19,2). Since a valid array has at most one agreement for each row pair, every pair agrees exactly once.",
  "Consequently each row meets exactly18 other rows. Starting from size3 in every column, its agreement-degree is16+t+e1+e2+e3, where t is its P0-quad membership count and each special-column e is+1 for a size4 block,0 for a size3 block,-1 for the size2 pair. Thus t+e1+e2+e3=2.",
  "For h, t=5, hence e1+e2+e3=-3. All three values must be-1, so h belongs to the pair in every special column.",
  "If a row has t=0 and belongs to a special pair, its three e values sum at most-1+1+1=1, contradicting the required sum2. Thus no empty row can belong to any special pair.",
  "A special pair containing h needs a different partner. Any nonempty row is forbidden because it already agrees with h in a P0 quad, and any empty row is forbidden by the preceding identity. No partner exists. Contradiction; this exact case has no valid array."
 ],
